{
  "image_name": "1f3c8929d93213633164a0116ece2408.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how engulfed material is processed inside phagocytes and highlights a special pathway called LC3-associated phagocytosis (LAP). On the left, a particle is internalized through dead-cell receptors, with actin helping form a phagosome. The compartment then matures in stages: an early phagosome marked by PI3P and RAB5, a later phagosome marked by RAB7 and acidification via V-ATPase, and finally fusion with lysosomes to make a phagolysosome. This mature compartment contains degradative enzymes that break down the cargo.\n\nThe right side shows how LAP modifies this process. A PI3K complex containing Rubicon, Beclin 1, VPS34, UVRAG, and VPS15 promotes recruitment of LC3 to the phagosome membrane. When LC3 is attached, the resulting LC3-positive phagosome appears to fuse rapidly with lysosomes, degrade cargo efficiently, and support immune-silent or anti-inflammatory outcomes. In contrast, when LAP is lost and the phagosome remains LC3-negative, lysosome fusion and cargo degradation are impaired, while inflammatory cytokine production increases. Overall, the figure compares standard phagosome maturation with LAP-enhanced maturation that links efficient clearance to reduced inflammation.",
